# Android services stubbed out on sargo because they only spam the journal
# under LuneOS/Halium (see meta-android's pre-start.d/50-stub-services).
#
# Pixel telemetry reporter; polls the never-present IStats framework service
# about once a second.
vendor/bin/pixelstats-vendor
# Waits for the Titan M strongbox keymaster, polling keymaster@3.0 hwservices
# that never register. init exec_starts it and waits, so the stub must exit.
vendor/bin/hw/wait_for_strongbox exit
# Titan M reboot-escrow HAL; polls the same keymaster services.
vendor/bin/hw/android.hardware.rebootescrow-service.citadel
# Display post-processing daemon; needs the framework sensorservice for the
# ambient light sensor and crash-loops without it.
vendor/bin/mm-pp-dpps
# SurfaceFlinger configstore HAL. Dies with SIGSYS - a seccomp kill - inside
# ISurfaceFlingerConfigs::registerAsService(), on a thread bionic spawns whose
# stack randomisation calls arc4random -> getentropy -> getrandom. It therefore
# never finishes registering, so nothing can ever reach it and stubbing it
# removes no capability: there is no surfaceflinger under Halium either, and the
# libgui/libui clients that would query it fall back to their compiled-in
# defaults. init restarts it every 5 seconds regardless, which on sargo produced
# 235 coredumps and 30 MB in the first 8 minutes of uptime - about 5.5 GB a day
# onto userdata. Measured, not estimated.
vendor/bin/hw/android.hardware.configstore@1.1-service
# On-device modem/diag loggers. diag_mdlog held DIAG_WS and the cdsp/lpass
# IPC-router ports awake and was the DIAG_WS suspend aborter on battery
# (measured 2026-09-19: DIAG_WS 7/min -> 0 with it stopped). Both are
# "disabled" in the vendor rc; init exec-starts diag_mdlog as a oneshot, so
# the stub must exit rather than sleep.
vendor/bin/diag_mdlog exit
vendor/bin/ssr_diag
# Android's battery health HAL. Nothing in LuneOS consumes it (batteryd and
# nyx read sysfs; LuneOS's own storaged is a different daemon outside the
# container); the only container consumer is Android's storaged, which polls
# hwservicemanager once a second when the HAL is absent, so both go. The
# kernel drivers do the charging; healthd only logged and armed a 60 s
# boot-time alarm that woke the SoC once a minute in suspend.
vendor/bin/hw/android.hardware.health@2.0-service.bonito
system/bin/storaged
